] [17592186078615 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186444253 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078615 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186444256 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078615 #edb/ref 1039 "Equivalent Expressions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078615 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186078160 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078615 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "48f63068-6837-522f-9268-efe63f9c2649" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078665 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186079589 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078665 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186079590 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078665 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186079591 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078665 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186079592 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078665 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186079593 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078665 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078665 #edb/ref 1045 "Identifying Terms in Linear Expressions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078665 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186079589 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078665 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050306 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078665 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078665 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "644d008d-9b6c-5a98-bd2e-9f7418e77c4f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078756 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186075420 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078756 #edb/ref 1069 "q-175713" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078756 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078756 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to the expression $\\frac{1}{3}b+2a?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078756 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cebb10af-299a-5836-8ba0-69ae27e2317d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078756 #edb/ref 1074 "The expression $\\frac{1}{3}b+2a$ has two terms: $\\frac{1}{3}b$ and $2a.$ \n\nThe order in which we combine the terms doesn't matter. Therefore, we can also write this expression as \n\n$$\n2a+\\frac{1}{3}b.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078914 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186076262 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078914 #edb/ref 1069 "q-43974" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078914 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078914 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to the expression $6z+\\frac{3}{2}x+2y+\\frac{5}{2}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078914 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4be44943-f72f-5283-9975-8fd399e1e267" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078914 #edb/ref 1074 "The expression $6z+\\frac{3}{2}x+2y+\\frac{5}{2}$ has four terms:  $6z$, $\\frac{3}{2}x$, $2y$ and $\\frac{5}{2}.$ \n\nThe order in which we combine the terms doesn't matter. Therefore, we can also write this expression as \n\n$$\n2y+\\frac{5}{2}+6z+\\frac{3}{2}x.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079175 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186077828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079175 #edb/ref 1069 "q-67052" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079175 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079175 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the fourth term of the expression $5a-\\frac{4}{3}b-12c+\\frac{1}{2}d?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079175 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d154a3f6-ea14-55ae-9f7c-cdadfdec6941" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079175 #edb/ref 1074 "We put an addition symbol $(+)$ between each part and separate using parentheses:\n\n$$\n(5a)+(-\\frac{4}{3}b)+(-12c)+(\\frac{1}{2}d)\n$$\n\nCounting off the terms:\n\n- the first term is $5a,$\n\n- the second term is $-\\frac{4}{3}b,$\n\n- the third term is $-12c,$ and\n\n- the fourth term is $\\frac{1}{2}d.$\n\nIn particular, we are interested in the fourth term. The fourth term is $\\frac{1}{2}d.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079589 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186079590 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079589 #edb/ref 1061 75175 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079589 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f8931686-8ab0-55f7-ac91-27963af09ae5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079589 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186079714 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079590 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186079591 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079590 #edb/ref 1061 75176 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079590 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d3ccacbe-b3b1-59d6-aca9-544e52d96477" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079590 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186078613 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079591 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186079592 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079591 #edb/ref 1061 75180 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079591 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a41ca648-ff16-5981-9555-fc4f21dbf835" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079591 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186078614 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079592 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186079593 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079592 #edb/ref 1061 75184 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079592 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "99d3eb64-ea42-5fb4-9098-6906b67605f3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079592 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186079715 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079593 #edb/ref 1061 75185 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079593 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d5df34ea-2918-56a4-bca6-901f97786756" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079593 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186078615 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079714 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's take a look at the following algebraic expression:\n\n$$\n2x{\\,\\color{red}{-}\\,} 4y {\\,\\color{blue}{+}\\,} 7\n$$\n\nThe addition $({\\color{blue}{+}})$ and subtraction $({\\color{red}{-}})$ symbols separate this expression into three parts known as **terms**.  \n\nTo identify each term, we put an addition symbol $({\\color{blue}{+}})$ between each part and separate using parentheses:\n\n$$\n(2x) {\\,\\color{blue}{+}\\,} ({\\,\\color{red}{-}\\,} 4y) {\\,\\color{blue}{+}\\,} (7).\n$$\n\nSo, the terms of this expression are: \n\n$$\n2x,\\quad -4y,\\quad  \\quad 7\n$$\n\nWe say that $2x$ is the **first term**, $-4y$ is the **second term**, and $7$ is the **third term**. \n\nIn this example, the subtraction symbol gave us the term $-4y$. Whenever there is a subtraction symbol, the corresponding term will be negative." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079714 #edb/ref 1065 8035 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079714 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a3fedc83-1abf-5dae-af95-68dd8069bf3e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079714 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079715 #edb/ref 1064 "When two algebraic expressions contain exactly the same terms, they are said to be **equivalent**.\n\nFor example, the expression\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{x}} + {\\color{red}{2y}} + 3z\n$$\n\nis equivalent to \n\n$$\n3z+{\\color{blue}{x}} + {\\color{red}{2y}}\n$$\n\nbecause they both contain the exact same terms. Both expressions are also equivalent to\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{x}} + 3z+ {\\color{red}{2y}}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079715 #edb/ref 1065 8036 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079715 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ad9444b4-97b9-501f-a587-b712d00315a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186079715 #edb/ref 1067 "Equivalent Expressions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444175 #edb/ref 1080 "xyz" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444175 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444175 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "029b656c-ed51-5555-9c8d-27587222dae8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444176 #edb/ref 1080 "$2d$, $-9e$, and $5f$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444176 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444176 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "03cd6056-ba55-56db-84a2-95b8cbd254de" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444177 #edb/ref 1080 "$p$, $2q$, and $3r$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444177 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444177 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0a763403-d8be-5f61-a2b2-6ad454e7dff4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444178 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{4}{5}b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186444178 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417]